! Norm-conserving silicon at dual = 8, so that the smooth and dense grids differ.
!
! Nothing here is ultrasoft: the pseudopotential, the density and the energies
! are the ones the norm-conserving path already reproduces to 1e-8 Ry. What the
! larger ecutrho changes is only the *plumbing* -- wavefunctions live on the
! smooth grid, the density and the potential on the dense one, and the potential
! has to be interpolated between them. That makes this the one case where the
! two-grid machinery can be validated on its own, with no augmentation charge,
! no S operator and no D-coefficient rebuild to confuse a discrepancy with.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=2, celldm(1)=10.20, nat=2, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0, ecutrho=96.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS
  2
   0.250000  0.250000  0.250000   1.00
   0.250000  0.250000  0.750000   3.00
